A||N||8||Eric_PA||2.8 rebuild with forgeg pistons||||5||
Z||000000||Eric_PA||03-07-2001||12:08 PM||||I am still trying to make my mind up on what engine to put into my GT. I am thinking about rebuilding a 2.8 with the basic performance items sold by the Fiero Store including the TRW Forged pistons. <P>1) Has anyone done this or know of how much power that could produce. <P>2) Would I be better off stroking the engine to a 3.1? <P>3) Is there much of a power difference between the two since Forged pistons aren't around for a 3.1 stroker, or are they?<P>I just don't want to jump back into getting another engine that never makes it into my Fiero and take a loss on it like I have recently done. The more I think about it, I should have stuck with the HO Quad. O-well   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Woops, I wanted to fix the title but can't Forged not forgeg  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Eric_PA (edited 03-07-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Steven||03-07-2001||02:14 PM||||You might want to go to Northern Auto Parts web site ( <A HREF="http://www.northernautoparts.com" TARGET=_blank>www.northernautoparts.com</A>  ). They sell an engine kit for the 2.8 that is half the price of the Fiero Store. The pistons are Seal Power, which is made by Federal Mogul the same company the makes TRW. They are pretty good about getting your stuff to you. I think I had my kit in 3-4 days.||||reg||1
Z||000002||HarryG||03-07-2001||03:13 PM||||Unless you're going to use a turbo or nitrous, I think using forged pistons would be overkill.  They require more clearance than cast ones, and are noisy on start-up until the engine warms up.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Matt88||03-07-2001||04:35 PM||||I agree with the response to forged pistons - generally these are only necessary for high compression, high heat or high RPM (constant RPM greater than 6500) purposes.<P>Cast pistons are quite durable, even in most performance applications.  If you are concerned, you could use some hyperutectic (sp?) pistons that generally cost less than forged; however I don't know what manufacturer makes "Hyper" pistons for the 2.8/3.1/3.4.<P>Also, if you have the money, go with the 3.1 crank and stroke.  The longer stroke should move the torque range into the lower RPM - something these V6's could use for daily driving as well as performance purposes.||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||03-07-2001||05:21 PM||||I recommend you build up the 2.8-liter engine and forget stroking it with a 3.1 crankshaft. The problem with using the stroked crankshaft is that you cannot get any pistons out there that will give you a high enough compression ratio to make decent power without getting custom pistons. Don't use OEM cast pistons in a high performance motor. There’re not really suitable for that kind you use although there are some people here that will tell you they are. Don't use OEM cast pistons. I would only use forged pistons if I were going to run nitrous or a turbocharger on the motor but you can use them if that is what you like, otherwise a high quality set of hypereutectic pistons is perfect. Don't buy anything from Federal Mogul. Their pistons are some of the worst quality pistons I have ever seen on the market. Do get Silvolite or Keith Black pistons. They are light, strong, and the quality is very good. Either way have the engine balanced. <P>If you use a crane 2030 camshaft with a 3.1-liter engine than your power output would be 156 horsepower @ 5000 rpm and 196 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3500 rpm. The 3.1-liter engine would have a compression ratio somewhere around 8.2-8.4 and the power will drop of quickly above 5000 rpm where the 2.8 will keep on pulling. See why here in one of my threads.  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008285.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008285.html</A>  A 2.8-liter engine with that same camshaft and 9.5-1 compression would make 165 horsepower @ 5500 rpm and 191-ft. lbs. of torque @ 3500 rpm. <P>With the 3.1-liter engine, a 2925-lb vehicle weight including you and the five-speed transmission that car would go 0-60 mph in 7.2 seconds and run the quarter mile in 15.23 seconds @ 88.6 mph. The same car with the high compression 2.8-liter engine would go 0-60 mph in 6.85 seconds and run the quarter mile in 15.0 seconds @ 91 mph and it would get better gas mileage to.<P><BR>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG">   <P>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 03-07-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 03-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||03-07-2001||05:27 PM||||I forgot to tell you to get ARP rod bolts and ARP main studs. Don't use the stock bolts if you want a strong bottom end.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Eric_PA||03-07-2001||06:52 PM||||Thanks so far for all the input. It is so hard for me to decide on what route to take. Along with my future plans to rebuild or swap an engine I am also converting to a Getrag 5 speed from an automatic. That just makes things a bit more messy <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>88formula.. I have been following that post and found it to be very informative. That post was one of the reasons for me to step back before jumping into a stroker rebuild. I was talking to some friends of mine that are into VW performance rebuilds. They tell me that I would be better off with the 2.8 because of the shorter stroke which would provide for a revier motor. I don't know if this would be true, but my guess would be that the closer the mass is to the crank the more responsive it would be.<P>I really appreciate all of your input on this, as this will be my first engine rebuild. I am very good with the wrench but will be going by the books and no experience. Kind of scary, but have to learn somehow. Looks like I will probably do a 2.8 performance build, but after I look into the 3.4 with the TDC pistons. Have to compare costs a bit, but looks like it will be between the two of them.<P>Eric<BR>Red 84 4 Speed<BR>Black 86.5 Fastback automatic (ratomatic)||||reg||7
Z||000007||G-Nasty||03-08-2001||12:24 AM||||I've done a rebuild using the fierostores forged pistons crane compucam. Go with .030 over and use ARP rod bolts. The biggest problem is adjusting valves. At TDC do not do what the Haynes or chiltons manuals say. Turn 1/4 or 1/2 turn after acheiving valve lash and not 1 and a half turns. 1 and 1/2 is overkill and your exhaust manifold will glow. The car did perform better but the valves are still loud. Good Luck-||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mr.Frosty||03-08-2001||10:23 AM||||Hey Guys,<BR>  My GT has a custom turbo setup,but the engine is shot.I was also looking at the rebuilds available,and trying to decide on<BR>kits.What is a good compression ratio for<BR>a turbo rebuild (lower or higher)8:5-1 is<BR>stock right.Mine rattles like a mother in<BR>boost.It is setup at 7psi with water meth<BR>inj.and extra fuel inj.I am currently trying to retime it and having much difficulty.I <BR>pulled the rotor and marked it before removal<BR>but after changing o-ring it is now totally out of whack.<P>------------------<BR>88 Formula T-top <BR>85 GT Turbo||||reg||11
